# =============================================================================
# $HOME as a git repository — deny by default.
#
# Git applies LAST MATCH WINS. This file is ordered in five blocks so that each
# one overrides the block above it:
#
#   1. ignore everything
#   2. keep directories traversable        (overrides 1)
#   3. allowlist — the only tracked paths  (overrides 2)
#   4. denylist — secrets and bulk         (overrides 3)
#   5. carve-outs inside block 4's bulk    (overrides 4, bulk only)
#
# To find out why a path is ignored:  git check-ignore -v <path>
# To track something new: add a block 3 entry, then `git add <path>` by name.
# Never loosen block 4's secret half — a path that looks like a credential stays
# ignored whatever else names it. Block 5 exists only because git will not
# re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, which makes a block 3
# entry for a path inside a bulk-ignored tree unreachable rather than merely
# outranked; see its own comment for when it may be extended.
# =============================================================================


# --- BLOCK 1: ignore everything ----------------------------------------------
# Nothing is tracked unless a block 3 entry names it. This also implements the
# rule that every root entry not starting with "." is ignored.
*

# --- BLOCK 2: keep directories traversable -----------------------------------
# Git will not re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded. Without
# this line, a nested entry like `!/.claude/settings.json` is silently inert.
# Block 4 re-ignores the heavy directories so the tree is still pruned.
!*/

# `!*/` above re-includes EVERY directory, which would defeat the rule that
# root entries not starting with "." are ignored (block 1 covers root files,
# but not root directories once `!*/` has run). Close that hole here, so
# `repos/` and friends are pruned. Block 3 re-includes `openspec/` explicitly.
/[!.]*/


# --- BLOCK 3: allowlist ------------------------------------------------------
# The complete set of tracked paths. Directory entries need `/**` — `!/dir/`
# re-includes only the directory, not the files inside it.

# this file
!/.gitignore

# shell
!/.bashrc
!/.profile
!/.bash_logout
!/.inputrc

# fish — named files rather than the whole directory. completions/ holds
# generated scripts that each tool reinstalls (openspec and herdr both write
# theirs), and fish_variables is where fish records universal variables, which
# are set on the machine rather than in a config file. Both are machine-local.
#
# The two completions named below are the exception that rule did not
# anticipate: waybar ships only a section-5 page describing its config file,
# and npx documents its options in prose, so fish derives nothing from either
# and both were written by hand. No tool regenerates them, so untracked means
# lost. They are named as files — re-including the directory would pull in the
# tool-written scripts the rule above deliberately excludes.
!/.config/fish/conf.d/**
!/.config/fish/functions/**
!/.config/fish/completions/waybar.fish
!/.config/fish/completions/npx.fish

# wayland session — the compositor's configuration and the terminal it opens.
# foot.ini is read once by `foot --server` when the session starts, so it is
# configuration and not state. The systemd user units that start that server
# are enabled on the machine and have no tracked file of their own.
# The two waybar files carry the bar's appearance, its geometry and its module
# lists. Each module's own options still come from /etc/xdg/waybar/config.jsonc,
# which config.jsonc includes rather than copies; the lists are restated because
# the packaged ones name sway modules that cannot start under niri.
!/.config/niri/config.kdl
!/.config/foot/foot.ini
!/.config/swaylock/config
!/.config/waybar/style.css
!/.config/waybar/config.jsonc

# The application launcher. Its `terminal` line, which is what desktop entries
# marked `Terminal=true` are launched through -- fuzzel's default names
# $TERMINAL, which this session does not set -- and its colours, which the
# package ships as Solarized Light in a session that is dark everywhere else.
!/.config/fuzzel/fuzzel.ini

# The Bluetooth and Wi-Fi menus, each run by its own launcher entry and by the
# bar icon beside it. They live here rather than in `.local/bin` because block
# 4 ignores `.local/` wholesale, and a block 3 entry cannot re-include a file
# whose parent directory block 4 excludes.
!/.config/fuzzel/fuzzel-bluetooth
!/.config/fuzzel/fuzzel-wifi

# The notification daemon. Its colours are the same Catppuccin Mocha the
# launcher and the bar restate, and its timeouts decide which of the Bluetooth
# and Wi-Fi menus' messages stay on screen.
!/.config/mako/config

# Font ordering, not fonts. The rule keeps Font Awesome from answering for
# emoji codepoints it declares but is not wanted for; the font files it names
# are installed by package and by hand, and are not tracked here.
!/.config/fontconfig/conf.d/*.conf

# git
# NOTE: .gitconfig is deliberately NOT tracked — identity and credential
# helpers differ per machine and per user. Set those locally instead.
!/.githooks/**

# tool configs
# direnv: the files, not `/.config/direnv/**`. Only the helper that project
# .envrc files call and direnv's own settings are tracked. direnv's approval
# records are per-machine and must never be carried to another one -- they are
# already covered by the `.local/` denylist entry in block 4, and naming the
# files here keeps the tracked set correct even on a direnv old enough to keep
# them under $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/direnv/ instead.
!/.config/direnv/direnvrc
!/.config/direnv/direnv.toml
!/.config/gh/config.yml
!/.config/herdr/config.toml
!/.config/herdr/herdr-equalize-toggle
!/.config/herdr/equalize-slots/**
!/.config/caveman/config.json

# vivid: the themes, not `/.config/vivid/**`. A theme is a colour scheme this
# repository chose and carries; the filetype database is vivid's own and is
# compiled into the binary, so anything else that lands in this directory came
# from the machine rather than from here.
!/.config/vivid/themes/*.yml

# xkb: the whole directory. Everything under it is a keyboard definition this
# repository wrote -- the custom symbols and the rules file that layers them
# over the system ones. libxkbcommon creates nothing here itself, so there is no
# machine-generated state to keep out.
!/.config/xkb/**

# kbd: the console keymap. Tracked here and read from nowhere -- it is the
# master copy of a file installed under /usr/lib/kbd/keymaps/, which is outside
# $HOME and so cannot be tracked in place. README.md carries the install step.
!/.config/kbd/*.map

# claude code — declaration only; the install tree is derived (see block 4)
!/.claude/settings.json
!/.claude/statusline-command.sh
!/.claude/commands/**
!/.claude/skills/**
!/.claude/agents/**
!/.claude/hooks/**

# neovim
!/.config/nvim/**

# README — stated exception to the "non-dot root entries are ignored" rule,
# so GitHub renders the bootstrap instructions on the repository page.
!/README.md

# openspec — stated exception to the "non-dot root entries are ignored" rule.
# Two lines: the first re-includes the directory (git will not descend into an
# excluded directory), the second re-includes its contents.
!/openspec/
!/openspec/**


# --- BLOCK 4: denylist -------------------------------------------------------
# Overrides block 3. A block 3 entry can never re-include anything matched here.
# The two `!` lines inside this block are narrow repairs to an over-broad
# pattern on the line just above them — they are part of the denylist, not
# escapes from it.

# -- secrets: named paths present in this $HOME --
.ssh/
.config/gh/hosts.yml
.claude/.credentials.json
.ghtoken
.claude.json
.bash_history
.psql_history
.viminfo

# -- secrets: patterns, for whatever appears later --
# Deliberately over-broad. A false positive costs one line of deliberation;
# a false negative costs a published key.
id_*
!*.pub
adbkey*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.crt
*.cer
.env
.env.*
*_history
*.history
*credential*
*secret*
*token*
.netrc
.npmrc
.pypirc

# -- git configuration: per-machine identity and credential helpers --
# Never tracked. Identity lives in .git/config (repo-local, uncommitted).
# Listed here as well as omitted from block 3, so that no directory allowlist
# entry can reintroduce one at any depth.
.gitconfig
.gitconfig.*
.gitconfig-*
**/.gitconfig
.config/git/
**/.git-credentials
.git-credentials

# -- telemetry and per-install identifiers --
# Machine-scoped analytics state. Publishing an "anonymous" id deanonymizes it,
# and syncing one makes every machine report as the same install.
.config/openspec/config.json
**/telemetry.json
**/.telemetry
**/machine-id
**/*machine-id*
.config/**/anonymousId

# -- WSL alternate-data-stream leftovers --
*:Zone.Identifier

# -- derived state: track the declaration, ignore what the tool rebuilds --
# .claude/settings.json carries extraKnownMarketplaces + enabledPlugins;
# Claude Code reconstructs .claude/plugins/ from it. The files here also record
# installLocation as absolute paths into this machine's home directory.
.claude/plugins/

# herdr plugins, the same story: README names the install command, and herdr
# rebuilds this tree from it. `plugins.json` records absolute paths, a
# content-hashed directory name, and an install timestamp, and each managed
# checkout carries its own .git, which git would otherwise offer as an
# embedded repository.
.config/herdr/plugins/
.config/herdr/plugins.json

# -- claude code: session and machine-local state --
.claude/projects/
.claude/sessions/
.claude/session-env/
.claude/file-history/
.claude/shell-snapshots/
.claude/paste-cache/
.claude/backups/
.claude/cache/
.claude/downloads/
.claude/daemon/
.claude/jobs/
.claude/tasks/
.claude/plans/
.claude/ide/
# /safe-clear writes its handoff notes here when the state does not fit in the
# resume line. One session's in-flight work, described in this machine's paths.
.claude/handoff/
.claude/history.jsonl
.claude/daemon.log
.claude/*.jsonl
.claude/*.json
!.claude/settings.json
.claude/settings.json.doctor-backup

# Machine-local settings layer. Merges over the tracked settings.json, and is
# where anything machine- or employer-specific belongs (autoMode.environment
# in particular) so the tracked file stays portable and publishable.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/.last-cleanup
.claude/.caveman-active
.claude/.caveman-nudge-shown

# -- bulk: caches, package stores, toolchains, editor server state --
# .android/ also holds adbkey, an RSA private key (see the patterns above).
.android/
.cache/
.npm/
.nuget/
.cargo/
.dotnet/
.nvm/
.vscode-server/
.ServiceHub/
.local/
.aspnet/
.templateengine/
.landscape/
.copilot/
.wget-hsts
.motd_shown


# --- BLOCK 5: carve-outs inside block 4's bulk trees --------------------------
# Block 3 is where a tracked path is named, and for anything outside block 4's
# bulk list that is where the entries below would go. It cannot hold them: git
# will not re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, so a block 3
# entry for a path under `.local/` is not outranked by block 4 — it is never
# consulted at all.
#
# Nothing here touches the secret half of block 4. `.local/` is on the bulk list,
# and that list exists to keep the repository small and its status fast, not to
# keep anything out of it. The rule this narrows is a size rule.
#
# Each ancestor is therefore re-included and its contents immediately re-excluded,
# rather than opening `.local/` and relying on the entries below to be the only
# matches. `.local/` holds 2.2 GB, and `.local/share/` holds `keyrings/`; the
# pattern pairs below are what keep git from descending into either while still
# reaching two directories that together hold 20 KB.
#
# Extend this block only for a path that cannot live anywhere else. These four
# qualify: the launcher reads desktop entries and icons from these directories
# and no others, because the paths are the XDG base directory specification's
# rather than a preference. The scripts they name are not here — those live in
# `.config/fuzzel/`, which block 3 reaches without any of this.
!/.local/
/.local/*
!/.local/bin/
/.local/bin/*
!/.local/share/
/.local/share/*
!/.local/share/applications/
/.local/share/applications/*
!/.local/share/icons/
/.local/share/icons/*
!/.local/share/icons/hicolor/
/.local/share/icons/hicolor/*
!/.local/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/
/.local/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/*
!/.local/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/
/.local/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/*

# The launcher entries for the Bluetooth and Wi-Fi menus, and the two icons they
# name. The icons are Adwaita's symbolic glyphs recoloured to the launcher's text
# colour: the originals are #2e3436 on a #1e1e2e background, which is a glyph
# that is present, correct and invisible.
!/.local/share/applications/bluetooth.desktop
!/.local/share/applications/wifi.desktop
!/.local/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/fuzzel-bluetooth.svg
!/.local/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/fuzzel-wifi.svg

# The symbolic links that put those two scripts on PATH. They are tracked, not
# derived, because the desktop entries above and the bar's click handlers name
# the scripts by bare command name rather than by path -- a desktop entry cannot
# say $HOME, and a tracked file naming this machine's home directory is the thing
# the ignore policy exists to keep out. The links carry relative targets for the
# same reason, so a clone into a differently named home resolves them.
!/.local/bin/fuzzel-bluetooth
!/.local/bin/fuzzel-wifi
